# {{PROJECT_NAME}}

This repository's product purpose and architecture belong to the project itself. ToVA Project Workflow generation 2 provides the supporting planning, documentation, verification, and continuity layer.

## Using the workflow

You describe what you want; your agent maintains the project documents. Start by confirming the project goal. Save ideas for later in Future Features or Future Capabilities without starting them. Plan substantial work as a Campaign made of manageable Slices; small tasks can be independent Slices, and trivial polish needs no formal plan. Once a plan is approved, say **"Do the next slice."** The agent follows Current Task, makes the bounded change, runs focused checks, updates the board, and reports the checkpoint. Broader checks belong at the relevant Campaign or release acceptance point, not every Slice.

For the main files with one-line descriptions, examples, and a short walkthrough, read `{{OWNER_tovaHelp}}` or ask **"Explain the workflow."** Agents should present a brief introduction once during setup or first use, then remember `shown` or your explicit `skipped` choice in the User Introduction section of `{{OWNER_currentState}}`. Asking for help always works, even after it has been shown or skipped. No quiz or extra approval is needed.

Existing repositories are migration sources, not alternate ToVA standards. The current supported Starter Kit defines the target ToVA workflow contract; preservation-first automation protects project information and user work while deliberate reconciliation adopts the current baseline. Preserve project truth and compatible local improvements, retain distinct declared extensions, and never retire a document before its unique current content has an accepted destination. Use `{{OWNER_tovaMigration}}` with Setup for established adoption or upgrades.

Start with:

- `{{OWNER_goals}}` for project purpose and success criteria
- `{{OWNER_architecture}}` for implementation structure and source ownership
- `{{OWNER_designLanguage}}` for reusable UI/source locations, typography and sizing, interaction patterns, terminology, and user-facing copy style
- `{{OWNER_currentCapabilities}}` for supported end-to-end outcomes and limitations
- `{{OWNER_currentFeatures}}` for concrete implemented mechanisms
- `{{OWNER_futureCapabilities}}` and `{{OWNER_futureFeatures}}` for desired outcomes and proposed mechanisms
- `{{OWNER_currentTask}}` for active work
- `{{OWNER_projectHealth}}` for real verification commands
- `{{OWNER_docsMap}}` for the complete document map
- `{{OWNER_tovaSetup}}` while deploying, initializing, adopting, or upgrading the workflow
- `{{OWNER_tovaMigration}}` during established-repository adoption or an older-version upgrade
- `{{OWNER_handoffs}}` when preparing or receiving a cross-AI, cross-session, or cross-machine transfer
- `{{OWNER_executionState}}` and `{{OWNER_collaborationProtocol}}` for durable execution state and online/local/multi-node coordination
- `{{OWNER_agentNotes}}` for sparse non-authoritative observations that must survive a session boundary
- `{{OWNER_outputs}}` when retaining a useful report, analysis, export, or other non-handoff work product
- `{{OWNER_projectDiscovery}}` while first-run project definition remains open; it asks five grouped questions and routes confirmed answers into the existing goal, architecture, state, task, and health owners

The workflow was initialized conservatively. Existing files were preserved; newly created truth files state what remains unknown instead of inventing product claims. If an answer is unclear, the agent should inspect repository evidence and offer two or three options plus one conservative default, but no suggestion becomes confirmed truth without the user's selection or confirmation.

Setup is complete only after every core owner is evaluated, target-local workflow/project/cold-start checks pass, acceptance evidence is saved, and installation identity is finalized. Optional overlays and modules remain conditional.
